<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/intermediate/metals-ceramics-polymers-composites">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The four families are <strong>four bonding-and-architecture strategies</strong>, each with a
    personality the periodic table hands it: metals bend, ceramics endure, polymers flex and
    shape, composites delegate. Materials engineering is choosing the family whose virtues your
    product needs and whose vices it can survive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four families and their signatures" widget="equationStep" config={familiesCfg} />

  <h2>Choosing among them in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cost per function beats cost per kilogram:</strong> aluminium costs 3× steel per kg but a third the density — per stiffness-requirement the ledger changes (the Ashby lesson\'s indices). Every "expensive material" argument is really a performance-index argument.</li>
    <li><strong>Polymers at temperature:</strong> plastics\' Tg and melting points limit service (a car interior endures 80 °C in sun); high-performance polymers (PEEK, polyimides) hold at 250 °C+ and fly in jet engines now — the family keeps climbing the engineering ladder.</li>
    <li><strong>Composites fail differently:</strong> sudden, delamination-prone, inspection-dependent (impacts leave invisible interior damage) — design and maintenance regimes must adapt to the material\'s failure personality (the next lessons\' fracture logic).</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental profiles differ sharply:</strong> steel\'s recycling dominance, concrete\'s 8% of global CO₂, polymers\' persistence, composites\' end-of-life difficulty (the wind-turbine-blade burial problem — the waste lesson\'s hardest case).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ashby, Materials Selection; Callister; the chemistry lesson\'s polymers chapter.</p>
</TopicPage>
